%% Chapter 42: Syntax-Directed Translation
\label{ch:syntax-directed-translation}

\section{Introduction to Syntax-Directed Translation}
\label{sec:sdt-intro}

Syntax-directed translation (SDT) is the process of translating a source program into an intermediate or target representation during parsing. Rather than constructing a concrete syntax tree and then transforming it in a separate pass, SDT interleaves translation with parsing: as the parser recognizes syntactic constructs, it simultaneously generates the corresponding output.

SDT is the bridge between the front end of a compiler (lexical and syntactic analysis) and the middle end (semantic analysis and intermediate code generation). It encompasses activities such as:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Building symbol tables (name resolution)
  \item Type checking and type inference
  \item Generating intermediate code (three-address code, bytecode, etc.)
  \item Performing simple optimizations (constant folding, strength reduction)
\end{itemize}

\begin{historical}
Syntax-directed translation was developed alongside the first compiler generators in the 1960s and 1970s. The concept of attaching semantic actions to grammar productions was implicit in early compiler implementations and was formalized by Knuth (1968) in his work on attribute grammars. The Yacc parser generator (Johnson, 1975) popularized the practice of embedding semantic actions directly in grammar productions. The textbook by Aho, Sethi, and Ullman (1986), later updated by Aho, Lam, Sethi, and Ullman (2007), established the standard terminology and techniques for syntax-directed translation.
\end{historical}

\section{Syntax-Directed Definitions}
\label{sec:sdd}

\subsection{Definition}
\label{sec:sdd-definition}

\begin{definition}[Syntax-Directed Definition]
\label{def:sdd}
A \textbf{syntax-directed definition} (SDD) is a context-free grammar together with:
\begin{itemize}
  \item A set of \textbf{attributes} associated with each grammar symbol
  \item A set of \textbf{semantic rules} (or \textbf{actions}) associated with each production
\end{itemize}
The semantic rules define how to compute attribute values from other attribute values.
\end{definition}

An SDD is an \emph{abstract} specification: it defines what attributes should be computed but not when or how they should be evaluated. The evaluation order is determined by the dependencies between attributes (as described in \cref{ch:attribute-grammars}).

\subsection{SDD for Arithmetic Expressions}
\label{sec:sdd-expr}

\begin{grammar}{SDD for Arithmetic Expressions}
  E & \longrightarrow E_1 + T \qquad & E.\textbf{val} = E_1.\textbf{val} + T.\textbf{val} \\
    & \longrightarrow T \qquad & E.\textbf{val} = T.\textbf{val} \\
  T & \longrightarrow T_1 \times F \qquad & T.\textbf{val} = T_1.\textbf{val} \times F.\textbf{val} \\
    & \longrightarrow F \qquad & T.\textbf{val} = F.\textbf{val} \\
  F & \longrightarrow ( E ) \qquad & F.\textbf{val} = E.\textbf{val} \\
    & \longrightarrow \textbf{num} \qquad & F.\textbf{val} = \textbf{num}.\textbf{val}
\end{grammar}

All attributes are synthesized, so this SDD can be evaluated during bottom-up parsing.

\subsection{SDD vs.\ Attribute Grammars}
\label{sec:sdd-vs-ag}

An SDD is essentially the same as an attribute grammar (\cref{ch:attribute-grammars}). The difference is primarily terminological:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Attribute grammars} emphasize the formal, mathematical properties (dependency graphs, evaluation order, circularity)
  \item \textbf{Syntax-directed definitions} emphasize the practical application to compiler construction
\end{itemize}

\section{Translation Schemes}
\label{sec:translation-schemes}

\subsection{Definition}
\label{sec:ts-definition}

\begin{definition}[Translation Scheme]
\label{def:ts}
A \textbf{translation scheme} (TS) is a context-free grammar with \textbf{semantic actions} (code fragments) embedded in the productions. The actions are executed at specific points during parsing.
\end{definition}

Unlike SDDs, which specify what to compute, translation schemes specify \emph{when} to compute it. The placement of actions within productions determines the evaluation order.

\subsection{Translation Scheme for Expressions}
\label{sec:ts-expr}

\begin{grammar}{Translation Scheme for Expression Translation}
  E & \longrightarrow \{ \texttt{gen("begin\_expr")} \} \; T \; E' \\
  E' & \longrightarrow + \; \{ \texttt{gen("push\_op, ADD")} \} \; T \; \{ \texttt{gen("emit ADD")} \} \; E' \\
     & \longrightarrow \epsilon \\
  T & \longrightarrow F \; T' \\
  T' & \longrightarrow \times \; \{ \texttt{gen("push\_op, MUL")} \} \; F \; \{ \texttt{gen("emit MUL")} \} \; T' \\
     & \longrightarrow \epsilon \\
  F & \longrightarrow ( \; E \; ) \\
    & \longrightarrow \textbf{num} \; \{ \texttt{gen("LOADI", num.val)} \}
\end{grammar}

The actions generate intermediate code as the expression is parsed.

\subsection{S-Attributed and L-Attributed Schemes}
\label{sec:s-l-schemes}

\begin{definition}[S-Attributed Translation Scheme]
\label{def:s-attr-ts}
A translation scheme is \textbf{S-attributed} if all attributes are synthesized. In this case, actions can be placed at the \emph{end} of each production (executed after all children have been parsed).
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[L-Attributed Translation Scheme]
\label{def:l-attr-ts}
A translation scheme is \textbf{L-attributed} if each inherited attribute depends only on inherited attributes of the parent and synthesized/inherited attributes of left siblings. In this case, actions can be placed at appropriate points within the production (before or between symbols).
\end{definition}

\section{Semantic Rules and Actions}
\label{sec:semantic-rules}

\subsection{Types of Semantic Actions}
\label{sec:action-types}

Semantic actions in a translation scheme can perform various tasks:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Attribute computation}: Compute the value of an attribute from other attributes.
  \item \textbf{Code generation}: Emit intermediate or target code.
  \item \textbf{Symbol table operations}: Enter or look up names in the symbol table.
  \item \textbf{Type checking}: Verify type compatibility and compute result types.
  \item \textbf{Error reporting}: Report semantic errors (type mismatches, undeclared variables).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Implementing Semantic Actions}
\label{sec:implementing-actions}

In a parser generator like Yacc/Bison, semantic actions are written as code fragments in the target language:

\begin{verbatim}
  expr: expr PLUS term
        { $$ = $1 + $3; }
      | term
        { $$ = $1; }
      ;
  term: term TIMES factor
        { $$ = $1 * $3; }
      | factor
        { $$ = $1; }
      ;
  factor: LPAREN expr RPAREN
          { $$ = $2; }
        | NUMBER
          { $$ = $1; }
        ;
\end{verbatim}

Here \texttt{\$\$} refers to the synthesized attribute of the left-hand side, and \texttt{\$1}, \texttt{\$2}, \texttt{\$3} refer to the attributes of the right-hand side symbols.

\subsection{Nonterminal Copying Rules}
\label{sec:copying-rules}

When a production has a single symbol on the right-hand side, the semantic rule is often a simple copy:

\begin{grammar}{Copying Rules}
  E & \longrightarrow T \qquad & E.\textbf{val} = T.\textbf{val} \\
  T & \longrightarrow F \qquad & T.\textbf{val} = F.\textbf{val}
\end{grammar}

In Yacc, this is written as \texttt{\{\ \$\$ = \$1; \}}.

\section{Intermediate Code Generation During Parsing}
\label{sec:icg}

\subsection{Three-Address Code}
\label{sec:three-address}

\emph{Three-address code} (TAC) is a common intermediate representation where each instruction has at most three operands:

\begin{definition}[Three-Address Instruction]
\label{def:tac}
A \textbf{three-address instruction} has the form:
\[
x = y \;\textbf{op}\; z
\]
where $x$, $y$, $z$ are operands (variables, constants, or temporaries) and \textbf{op} is an operator.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Generating TAC During Parsing}
\label{sec:tac-generation}

TAC can be generated during parsing using synthesized attributes to track the ``result'' of each expression:

\begin{grammar}{TAC Generation for Expressions}
  E & \longrightarrow E_1 + T \qquad & \begin{array}{l} E.\textbf{code} = E_1.\textbf{code} \concat T.\textbf{code} \\ \concat \texttt{gen}(E.\textbf{place} \texttt{=} E_1.\textbf{place} \texttt{+} T.\textbf{place}) \end{array} \\
    & \longrightarrow T \qquad & E.\textbf{code} = T.\textbf{code}; \; E.\textbf{place} = T.\textbf{place} \\
  T & \longrightarrow T_1 \times F \qquad & \begin{array}{l} T.\textbf{code} = T_1.\textbf{code} \concat F.\textbf{code} \\ \concat \texttt{gen}(T.\textbf{place} \texttt{=} T_1.\textbf{place} \texttt{*} F.\textbf{place}) \end{array} \\
    & \longrightarrow F \qquad & T.\textbf{code} = F.\textbf{code}; \; T.\textbf{place} = F.\textbf{place} \\
  F & \longrightarrow ( E ) \qquad & F.\textbf{code} = E.\textbf{code}; \; F.\textbf{place} = E.\textbf{place} \\
    & \longrightarrow \textbf{id} \qquad & F.\textbf{code} = \epsilon; \; F.\textbf{place} = \textbf{id}.\textbf{name} \\
    & \longrightarrow \textbf{num} \qquad & F.\textbf{code} = \epsilon; \; F.\textbf{place} = \textbf{num}.\textbf{val}
\end{grammar}

\subsection{Boolean Expressions and Control Flow}
\label{sec:boolean-expressions}

Boolean expressions require special handling because they involve control flow (branches):

\begin{grammar}{TAC Generation for Boolean Expressions}
  B & \longrightarrow B_1 \textbf{ or } B_2 \qquad & \begin{array}{l} B_1.\textbf{true} = B.\textbf{true}; \; B_1.\textbf{false} = \text{newlabel} \\ B_2.\textbf{true} = B.\textbf{true}; \; B_2.\textbf{false} = B.\textbf{false} \\ B.\textbf{code} = B_1.\textbf{code} \concat \texttt{gen}(B_1.\textbf{false} \texttt{:}) \concat B_2.\textbf{code} \end{array} \\
    & \longrightarrow \textbf{id}_1 \textbf{ < } \textbf{id}_2 \qquad & \begin{array}{l} B.\textbf{code} = \texttt{gen}(\texttt{if } \textbf{id}_1 \texttt{ < } \textbf{id}_2 \texttt{ goto } B.\textbf{true}) \\ \concat \texttt{gen}(\texttt{goto } B.\textbf{false}) \end{array}
\end{grammar}

The attributes \textbf{true} and \textbf{false} are labels to which control flows when the expression is true or false, respectively.

\subsection{Statements and Backpatching}
\label{sec:backpatching}

For statements like \texttt{if} and \texttt{while}, the target of branch instructions is not known until later in the parse. \emph{Backpatching} defers the filling in of branch targets until the target label is known.

\begin{definition}[Backpatching]
\label{def:backpatching}
\textbf{Backpatching} is the technique of generating branch instructions with placeholder targets and filling in the correct targets later, when they become known. A \emph{list} of instructions with unresolved targets is maintained and patched when the target label is determined.
\end{definition}

\begin{grammar}{Backpatching for If-Then-Else}
  S & \longrightarrow \textbf{if } ( B ) \; M \; S_1 \; N \; \textbf{else } S_2 \\
  M & \longrightarrow \epsilon \qquad & M.\textbf{quad} = \texttt{nextquad} \\
  N & \longrightarrow \epsilon \qquad & \begin{array}{l} \texttt{gen}(\texttt{goto } \_) \\ N.\textbf{quad} = \texttt{nextquad} \end{array}
\end{grammar}

The semantic actions generate branch instructions and backpatch the targets:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $B.\textbf{false}$ is backpatched to $M.\textbf{quad}$ (the start of the else branch)
  \item $N.\textbf{quad}$'s goto target is backpatched to the instruction after $S_2$
\end{itemize}

\section{Type Checking During Parsing}
\label{sec:type-checking}

\subsection{Type Attributes}
\label{sec:type-attributes}

Type checking is performed by computing a \textbf{type} attribute for each expression:

\begin{grammar}{Type Checking}
  E & \longrightarrow E_1 + E_2 \qquad & \begin{array}{l} \textbf{if } E_1.\textbf{type} = \textbf{int} \land E_2.\textbf{type} = \textbf{int} \\ \textbf{then } E.\textbf{type} = \textbf{int} \\ \textbf{else } E.\textbf{type} = \textbf{type\_error} \end{array} \\
    & \longrightarrow E_1 = E_2 \qquad & \begin{array}{l} \textbf{if } E_1.\textbf{type} = E_2.\textbf{type} \\ \textbf{then } E.\textbf{type} = \textbf{bool} \\ \textbf{else } E.\textbf{type} = \textbf{type\_error} \end{array} \\
    & \longrightarrow \textbf{id} \qquad & E.\textbf{type} = \texttt{lookup}(\textbf{id}.\textbf{entry}) \\
    & \longrightarrow \textbf{num} \qquad & E.\textbf{type} = \textbf{int}
\end{grammar}

\subsection{Type Coercion}
\label{sec:type-coercion}

When types don't match exactly but can be converted, the SDD generates \emph{coercion} operations:

\begin{grammar}{Type Coercion}
  E & \longrightarrow E_1 + E_2 \qquad & \begin{array}{l} \textbf{if } E_1.\textbf{type} = \textbf{int} \land E_2.\textbf{type} = \textbf{float} \\ \textbf{then } \begin{array}{l} E.\textbf{code} = E_1.\textbf{code} \concat \texttt{gen}(t \texttt{= inttofloat } E_1.\textbf{place}) \\ \concat E_2.\textbf{code} \concat \texttt{gen}(E.\textbf{place} \texttt{=} t \texttt{+} E_2.\textbf{place}) \end{array} \end{array}
\end{grammar}

\section{Building Symbol Tables During Parsing}
\label{sec:symbol-tables}

\subsection{Symbol Table Structure}
\label{sec:symbol-table-structure}

A symbol table maps identifiers to their attributes (type, scope, storage location, etc.). During parsing, the symbol table is built incrementally:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Enter}: When a declaration is parsed, the declared identifier is entered into the symbol table.
  \item \textbf{Lookup}: When an identifier is used, it is looked up in the symbol table.
  \item \textbf{Scope management}: When a scope is entered, a new level is pushed; when a scope is exited, the level is popped.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Scope Management}
\label{sec:scope-management}

Block-structured languages require nested scopes. The symbol table is organized as a stack of scopes:

\begin{grammar}{Scope Management}
  S & \longrightarrow \{ \texttt{mktable(null)} \} \; S_1 \; \{ \texttt{enter(scope\_stack, prev\_table)} \} \\
  D & \longrightarrow \textbf{var } \textbf{id} : T \qquad & \{ \texttt{enter(current\_table, id.name, T.type)} \}
\end{grammar}

The action \texttt{mktable(null)} creates a new symbol table with the current table as its parent. The action \texttt{enter} adds a binding to the current table.

\subsection{Name Resolution}
\label{sec:name-resolution}

When an identifier is used, it must be resolved to a declaration. The resolution process searches the symbol table from the innermost scope outward:

\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Function{Lookup}{$name$}
  \State $t \gets$ current table
  \While{$t \neq$ null}
    \If{$name \in t$}
    \State \textbf{return} $t[name]$
    \EndIf
    \State $t \gets t.\texttt{parent}$
  \EndWhile
  \State \textbf{return} \texttt{undeclared}
\EndFunction
\end{algorithmic}

\section{Practical Considerations}
\label{sec:sdt-practical}

\subsection{Integrating with Parser Generators}
\label{sec:sdt-generators}

Most parser generators support semantic actions:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Yacc/Bison}: Actions are C/C++ code fragments embedded in productions
  \item \textbf{ANTLR}: Actions are target-language code; also supports attribute grammars
  \item \textbf{JavaCC}: Actions are Java code embedded in productions
  \item \textbf{Menhir}: Actions are OCaml code
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Managing Complexity}
\label{sec:managing-complexity}

As the translation logic grows, embedding actions directly in productions becomes unwieldy. Strategies for managing complexity include:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Separate passes}: Separate parsing from semantic analysis (parse to AST, then analyze)
  \item \textbf{Visitor pattern}: Use the visitor pattern to traverse the AST and perform semantic analysis
  \item \textbf{Attribute grammar tools}: Use tools like JastAdd or Kiama that separate the grammar from the semantic rules
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Error Handling in Translation}
\label{sec:sdt-errors}

Semantic errors (type mismatches, undeclared variables) must be reported without halting the compilation. Common strategies include:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Error types}: Use a special \texttt{type\_error} type that propagates through expressions, suppressing cascading errors.
  \item \textbf{Error counts}: Count errors and continue; report the total at the end.
  \item \textbf{Error recovery}: Insert placeholder types or values to allow parsing to continue.
\end{itemize}

\section{Summary}
\label{sec:sdt-summary}

Syntax-directed translation is the process of generating output (intermediate code, symbol table entries, type annotations) during parsing. Key concepts include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Syntax-directed definitions (SDDs) as abstract specifications of translation
  \item Translation schemes (TSs) as concrete specifications with embedded actions
  \item S-attributed and L-attributed definitions for bottom-up and top-down parsing
  \item Intermediate code generation (three-address code, backpatching)
  \item Type checking and coercion during parsing
  \item Symbol table construction and name resolution
\end{itemize}

\begin{historical}
Syntax-directed translation has been a core technique in compiler construction since the earliest compilers. Key milestones include: the use of semantic actions in early compilers (1950s--1960s); Knuth's attribute grammars (1968); Yacc's embedded actions (Johnson, 1975); the Aho--Ullman textbook treatment (1977, 1986); backpatching techniques (Aho, Sethi, and Ullman, 1986); and modern tools that separate grammar from semantics (JastAdd, Kiama, ANTLR's visitor pattern). The tension between embedded actions (flexibility, performance) and separate specifications (modularity, maintainability) continues to shape the design of compiler front ends.
\end{historical}

